problem:
Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

initial_solution:
To expand and simplify the given expression, I'll start by following the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS) and then simplifying further.

Step 1: Distribute the terms inside the parentheses using the distributive property.
\begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x\left(x+x^2+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 2: Combine like terms inside the first set of parentheses.
\begin{align*}
x\left(x+x^2+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x\left(x^2 + 2x + x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 3: Simplify the expression inside the first set of parentheses by combining like terms.
\begin{align*}
x\left(x^2 + 2x + x^2\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x\left(2x^2 + 2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 4: Distribute the x term to the terms inside the parentheses.
\begin{align*}
x\left(2x^2 + 2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= 2x^3 + 2x^2 -3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Step 5: Distribute the -3 term to the terms inside the parentheses.
\begin{align*}
2x^3 + 2x^2 -3(x^2-x+2) &= 2x^3 + 2x^2 -3x^2 + 3x -6
\end{align*}

Step 6: Combine like terms in the expression.
\begin{align*}
2x^3 + 2x^2 -3x^2 + 3x -6 &= 2x^3 -x^2 + 3x -6
\end{align*}

The answer is \boxed{2x^3 -x^2 + 3x -6}.

