To find the angle $\angle ABC$, we will use the law of cosines.

Step 1: Find the lengths of the sides $AB, BC,$ and $CA$.
\[AB = \sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2 = \sqrt{(1-(-3))^2+(-4-(-11))^2 = \sqrt{16+196}=\sqrt{212}\]
\[BC = \sqrt{(x_3-x_2)^2+(y_3-y_2)^2 = \sqrt{(-2-(-3))^2+(\text{-}1-(-4))^2 = \sqrt{1+25}=\sqrt{26}\]
\[CA = \sqrt{(x_1-x_3)^2+(y_1-y_3)^2 = (-3-1)^2+(-4-(-11))^2 = 9+121 = 130\]

Step 2: Compute the dot product of vectors $\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\overrightarrow{BC}$.
\[\begin{aligned} \overrightarrow{BA} \cdot \overrightarrow{BC} &amp;= (\overrightarrow{BA}_x \cdot \overrightarrow{BC}_x + \overrightarrow{BA}_y \cdot \overrightarrow{BC}_y + \overrightarrow{BA}_z \cdot \overrightarrow{BC}_z) \\&amp;= (-2 \cdot -5 + -7 \cdot 5 + 1 \cdot -2)\\&amp;=10-35-2=-27\end{aligned}

Step 3: Compute the magnitudes of vectors $\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\overrightarrow{BC}$.
\begin{aligned}
|\overrightarrow{BA}| &= \sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-7)^2 + 1^2} \\&=\sqrt{4+49+1}\\&=\sqrt{54}
|\overrightarrow{BC}| &= \sqrt{(-5)^2 + 5^2 + (-2)^2} \\&=\sqrt{25+25+4}\\&=\sqrt{54}
\end{aligned}

Step 4: Use the law of cosines to find $\cos(\angle ABC)$.
\begin{aligned}
\cos(\angle ABC)&amp;=\frac{\overrightarrow{BA} \cdot \overrightarrow{BC}}{|\overrightarrow{BA}||\overrightarrow{BC}|} \\
&amp;=\frac{-27}{\sqrt{54}\sqrt{54}}\\
&amp;=\frac{-27}{54}\\
&amp;=-\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}

Step 5: Recall that the range of the arccosine function is from 0 to 180 degrees.
The cosine of an angle between 0 and 180 degrees is negative when the angle is greater than 90 degrees.
Since $\cos(\angle ABC)=-\frac{1}{2}$, $\angle ABC$ must be greater than 90 degrees.

Step 6: Find the measure of $\angle ABC$.
\begin{aligned}
\cos^{-1}\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)&amp;=60^\circ\\
\Rightarrow\quad\angle ABC&amp;=180^\circ-60^\circ\\
&amp;=\boxed{120}
\end{aligned}

The answer is \boxed{120}.

