To find the minimum value of the expression \[\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1},\] we first expand the numerator to get a simpler expression.

\begin{align*}
\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1} &= \frac{x^2 + 7x + 10}{x + 1}.
\end{align*}

Now, we want to rewrite this expression as a quotient of two polynomials. To do this, we can use polynomial long division.

\begin{array}{c|ccc}
x &amp; &amp; 10 \\
x+1 &\enclose{longdiv}{x^2 + 7x + 10}\pha \\
&\underline{\phantom{x^2}x^2 + x}\\
&amp;\underline{-4x\phantom{-4}}\\
&amp;\underline{-10}
\end{array}

Thus, we can write \begin{align*}
\frac{x^2 + 7x + 10}{x + 1} &= \frac{(x^2 + x) + (6x + 10)}{x + 1}\\
&= x + \frac{6(x + 10)}{x + 1}\\
&= x + \frac{6(x + 1) + \frac{4}{x + 1}}\\
&= x + 6 + \frac{4}{x + 1}.
\end{align*}Now, we note that the minimum value of the expression will occur when the denominator $x + 1$ is as small as possible, since the numerator is always positive.

The smallest value of $x + 1$ is 2, which occurs when $x = 1$. Plugging this value into the expression, we get\begin{align*}
1 + 6 + \frac{4}{2} &= 7 + 2\\
&= \boxed{9}
\end{align*}

The answer is \boxed{9}.

